UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 38, Lesson 2: Preparing the display and programme', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A showcase needs a programme and a display. A programme lists the items in order with titles and names, and may include a short welcome and a thank-you. A title card names the theme and the artist. Display artworks at eye level with space between them, and arrange them so the audience can follow a path. Use clear lettering, enough contrast between text and background and correct spelling. Set up walkways clear and keep chairs and equipment safe. Ask for help to carry or hang anything heavy.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWrite a three-item programme for a showcase with a welcome and a thank-you. Say one thing you would do so it is easy to read.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Welcome. 1. Seasons Poster Display. 2. Song of Four Seasons (class). 3. Autumn Dance (Zoe, Sam). Thank you for coming. I would use dark writing on light paper and check names.'' It has an order, a welcome, a thank-you and clear lettering.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking that a display just means putting artworks anywhere on a wall. Arranging works at eye level with space and labels helps the audience see and understand each one.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nDesign a programme and a display plan for your showcase with all items, names, a welcome and a thank-you. Check spelling with an adult. Draw the room layout showing walkways, seats and where the artworks go.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"A showcase needs a programme and a display. A programme lists the items in order with titles and names, and may include a short welcome and a thank-you. A title card names the theme and the artist. Display artworks at eye level with space between them, and arrange them so the audience can follow a path. Use clear lettering, enough contrast between text and background and correct spelling. Set up walkways clear and keep chairs and equipment safe. Ask for help to carry or hang anything heavy.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Create and perform","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-VIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Design a programme and a display plan for your showcase with all items, names, a welcome and a thank-you. Check spelling with an adult. Draw the room layout showing walkways, seats and where the artworks go. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Welcome. 1. Seasons Poster Display. 2. Song of Four Seasons (class). 3. Autumn Dance (Zoe, Sam). Thank you for coming. I would use dark writing on light paper and check names.'' It… Reasoning: I used a programme by attending to a display rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking that a display just means putting artworks anywhere on a wall. Arranging works at eye level with space and labels helps the audience see and… Correction: Strong answer: ''Welcome. 1. Seasons Poster Display. 2. Song of Four Seasons (class). 3. Autumn Dance (Zoe, Sam). Thank you for coming. I would use dark… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a programme.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking that a display just means putting artworks anywhere on a wall. Arranging works at eye level with space and labels helps the audience see and understand each one. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a programme on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a programme, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Welcome. 1. Seasons Poster Display. 2. Song of Four Seasons (class). 3. Autumn Dance (Zoe, Sam). Thank you for coming. I would use dark… This matches the lesson skill for a programme.","options":["Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''Welcome. 1. Seasons Poster Display. 2. Song of Four Seasons (class). 3. Autumn Dance (Zoe, Sam). Thank you for coming. I would use dark…","Thinking that a display just means putting artworks anywhere on a wall. Arranging works at eye level with space and labels helps the audience see and…","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Write a three-item programme for a showcase with a welcome and a thank-you. Say one thing you would do so it is easy to read. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Thinking that a display just means putting artworks anywhere on a wall. Arranging works at eye level with space and labels helps the audience see and… That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Thinking that a display just means putting artworks anywhere on a wall. Arranging works at eye level with space and labels helps the audience see and…","A showcase needs a programme and a display. A programme lists the items in order with titles and names, and may include a short welcome and a thank-you. A…","Strong answer: ''Welcome. 1. Seasons Poster Display. 2. Song of Four Seasons (class). 3. Autumn Dance (Zoe, Sam). Thank you for coming. I would use dark…","Careful use of a display with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising a programme?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named a display, not by copying the model.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the a display used."],"question":"A learner is practising a programme. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Welcome. 1. Seasons Poster Display. 2. Song of Four Seasons (class). 3. Autumn Dance (Zoe, Sam). Thank you for coming. I would use dark… uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''Welcome. 1. Seasons Poster Display. 2. Song of Four Seasons (class). 3. Autumn Dance (Zoe, Sam). Thank you for coming. I would use dark…","Thinking that a display just means putting artworks anywhere on a wall. Arranging works at eye level with space and labels helps the audience see and…","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about a programme?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Thinking that a display just means putting artworks anywhere on a wall. Arranging works at eye level with space and labels helps the audience see and…","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about a programme.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about a display?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Example: for the ''Water'' showcase, Mia designs a programme: 1. Wave Collage Display. 2. Rain Rhythm (Cleo, Dev). 3. Drought Drama (Ava,…","Thinking that a display just means putting artworks anywhere on a wall. Arranging works at eye level with space and labels helps the audience see and…","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — A showcase needs a programme and a display. A programme lists the items in order with titles and names, and… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Thinking that a display just means putting artworks anywhere on a wall. Arranging works at eye level with space and labels helps the audience see and…","Design a programme and a display plan for your showcase with all items, names, a welcome and a thank-you. Check spelling with an adult. Draw the…","Retype the worked example with no new example."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of a programme?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a programme on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Welcome. 1. Seasons Poster Display. 2. Song of Four Seasons (class). 3. Autumn Dance (Zoe, Sam). Thank you for coming. I would use dark writing on light paper and check names.'' It… Reasoning: I used a programme by attending to a display rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with a programme, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking that a display just means putting artworks anywhere on a wall. Arranging works at eye level with space and labels helps the audience see and… Correction: Strong answer: ''Welcome. 1. Seasons Poster Display. 2. Song of Four Seasons (class). 3. Autumn Dance (Zoe, Sam). Thank you for coming. I would use dark… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a programme.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w38-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a programme.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Creative Arts Showcase","success_criteria":["I can use a programme with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a display and explain why it matters.","I can practise a title card independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a welcome sign needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A showcase needs a programme and a display. A programme lists the items in order with titles and names, and may include a short welcome and a thank-you. A title card names the theme and the artist. Display artworks at eye level with space between them, and arrange them so the…","options":["A showcase needs a programme and a display. A programme lists the items in order with titles and names, and may include a short welcome and a thank-you. A title card names the theme and the artist. Display artworks at eye level with space between them, and arrange them so the audience can follow a path. Use clear lettering, enough contrast between text and background and correct spelling. Set up walkways clear and keep chairs and equipment safe. Ask for help to carry or hang anything heavy.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a programme?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: for the ''Water'' showcase, Mia designs a programme: 1. Wave Collage Display. 2. Rain Rhythm (Cleo, Dev). 3. Drought Drama (Ava, Sam). 4. Flowing Dance (Mia, Ben). Thank you for coming. She uses dark blue text on pale paper so it is easy to read, checks the spelling of each name and arranges the collage at eye level with a title card beside each.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite a three-item programme for a showcase with a welcome and a thank-you. Say one thing you would do so it is easy to read.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Welcome. 1. Seasons Poster Display. 2. Song of Four Seasons (class). 3. Autumn Dance (Zoe, Sam). Thank you for coming. I would use dark writing on light paper and check names.'' It has an order, a welcome, a thank-you and clear lettering.","explain":"A showcase needs a programme and a display. A programme lists the items in order with titles and names, and may include a short welcome and a thank-you. A title card names the theme and the artist. Display artworks at eye level with space between them, and arrange them so the audience can follow a path. Use clear lettering, enough contrast between text and background and correct spelling. Set up walkways clear and keep chairs and equipment safe. Ask for help to carry or hang anything heavy.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that a display just means putting artworks anywhere on a wall. Arranging works at eye level with space and labels helps the audience see and understand each one. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"A showcase programme.","caption":"Easy to read.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Programme</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">items, names, thanks</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Lettering</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">dark on light, spelled right</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: for the ''Water'' showcase, Mia designs a programme: 1. Wave Collage Display. 2. Rain Rhythm (Cleo, Dev). 3. Drou Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: for the ''Water'' showcase, Mia designs a programme: 1. Wave Collage Display. 2. Rain Rhythm (Cleo, Dev). 3. Drou Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a programme?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: for the ''Water'' showcase, Mia designs a programme: 1. Wave Collage Display. 2. Rain Rhythm (Cleo, Dev). 3. Drought Drama (Ava, Sam). 4. Flowing Dance (Mia, Ben). Thank you for coming. She uses dark blue text on pale paper so it is easy to read, checks the spelling of each name and arranges the collage at eye level with a title card beside each. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"A safe gallery display.","caption":"Space and safety.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Display</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">eye level, spaced out</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Walkway</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">kept clear and safe</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a programme?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking that a display just means putting artworks anywhere on a wall. Arranging works at eye level with space and labels helps the audience see and understand each one. Here is a more helpful example: Example: for the ''Water'' showcase, Mia designs a programme: 1. Wave Collage Display. 2. Rain Rhythm (Cleo, Dev). 3. Drought Drama (Ava, Sam). 4. Flowing Dance (Mia, Ben). Thank you for coming. She uses dark blue text on pale paper so it is easy to read, checks the spelling of each name and arranges the collage at eye level with a title card beside each.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Welcome. 1. Seasons Poster Display. 2. Song of Four Seasons (class). 3. Autumn Dance (Zoe, Sam). Thank you for coming. I would use dark writing on light paper and check names.'' It has an order, a welcome, a thank-you and clear lettering.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Thinking that a display just means putting artworks anywhere on a wall. Arranging works at eye level with space and labels helps the audience see and understand each one. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a programme?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA showcase needs a programme and a display. A programme lists the items in order with titles and names, and may include a short welcome and a thank-you. A title card names the theme and the artist. Display artworks at eye level with space between them, and arrange them so the audience can follow a path. Use clear lettering, enough contrast between text and background and correct spelling. Set up walkways clear and keep chairs and equipment safe. Ask for help to carry or hang anything heavy. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 38, Lesson 2: Preparing the display and programme","unit_number":8,"unit_title":"Creative showcase","week":38,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: for the ''Water'' showcase, Mia designs a programme: 1. Wave Collage Display. 2. Rain Rhythm (Cleo, Dev). 3. Drought Drama (Ava, Sam). 4. Flowing Dance (Mia, Ben). Thank you for coming. She uses dark blue text on pale paper so it is easy to read, checks the spelling of each name and arranges the collage at eye level with a title card beside each.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"A showcase programme.","caption":"Easy to read.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Programme</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">items, names, thanks</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Lettering</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">dark on light, spelled right</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"A safe gallery display.","caption":"Space and safety.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Display</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">eye level, spaced out</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Walkway</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">kept clear and safe</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y3-w38-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
